        </UserObject>
        <mxCell id="KKWmFWuTmSYwAzUDTlM0-1" parent="dIccFLpEfjdW09Kl_i50-1" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;overflow=hidden;rounded=0;" value="&lt;h1 style=&quot;margin-top: 0px;&quot;&gt;About&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;p&gt;“The flow begins when we give the Council a task and select the development workspace. The launcher starts the Python engine, which resolves the workspace, Council configuration, and private memory location.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The engine then determines whether this is a new run or a continuation. A new run receives a unique identifier and an initial state. A resumed run loads its saved checkpoint and verifies that it belongs to the same workspace and runtime context.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;For a new task, the first LangGraph node is the Architect. The GPT orchestrator turns the requirement into a structured plan: which specialists are needed, what each task should accomplish, which files it may affect, and which commands will verify the work. The engine validates those specialists, paths, and commands before proceeding.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;That brings us to Gate 1, the plan approval checkpoint. We review the proposed work and verification commands before execution. If approved, the graph moves forward. If rejected, the run ends as failed. If approval is unavailable, the engine can pause and save its place so we can resume later.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Next, the worker loads the instructions for the current specialist and connects the active model to that specialist’s permitted tools. Those tools can read, write, or patch workspace files and run supported checks. Tool access varies by specialist. The worker exchanges requests and results with the model, with a limit of twelve model rounds for each worker invocation.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;When the worker finishes, the Prover executes every verification command approved in the plan. This step uses real subprocesses and records their exit codes. Where the output provides recognizable test counts, those counts are recorded as well.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;If verification passes, the engine marks the task complete. It then advances to the next task and repeats the worker and verification steps. Tasks execute sequentially, so each task completes its verification before the next begins.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;If verification fails, the graph enters its repair loop. The Gemini investigator examines the recorded failure and a bounded portion of the actual command output. It produces a diagnosis that the worker can use to make a repair.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The worker normally uses the GPT orchestrator for that repair. If optional escalation is enabled, the second repair retry uses the configured Claude escalation model. Each task has an initial verification attempt followed by up to two repair retries. If those retries are exhausted, the engine stops and reports failure.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Once all planned tasks pass verification, the Gatekeeper runs the workspace integrity validator. A failed audit ends the run. A successful audit brings us to Gate 2, where we review and approve the pre-submit result.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Approval at Gate 2 marks the run complete. Completion means the planned tasks passed their verification commands, the workspace audit passed, and the final approval was granted. Committing changes, opening a pull request, and deploying remain separate actions.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Supporting this entire flow is the shared Council state and a durable SQLite checkpointer. They preserve the plan, task progress, verification evidence, retry counts, and approval interrupts. That allows a paused run to continue from its saved position rather than starting over.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Finally, every graph node checks the run’s identity and workspace context and enforces a step budget. These controls help keep execution bounded and tied to the workspace selected at the beginning.”&lt;/p&gt;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="750" width="820" x="90" y="286" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
</mxfile>
